/*
RMSTEST_23 - border_fuzziness penetration depth
preview-design.md Sec.15 item 1, Sec.6.1's border rejection

QUESTION
border_fuzziness is an adherence percentage, default 20. Sec.6.1 specifies it
as one Bernoulli(f/100) rejection roll PER TILE OF DEPTH beyond the border, so
a candidate d tiles out draws d rolls and is rejected if any succeeds.
Penetration then decays geometrically with a mean of a few tiles at the
default, and the edge comes out ragged.

That form was chosen over rev 3's single depth-independent coin flip because
the flat version accepts 80 percent of the first ring past the border AND 80
percent of the ring past that, which dissolves the border rather than fuzzing
it. The correction is sound reasoning about a distribution and it is still only
reasoning. Nobody has looked at a real fuzzed edge and counted.

METHOD
Four lands, identical in every way except border_fuzziness, each pinned to its
own horizontal strip so they cannot interfere, each with left_border 40 and a
budget big enough that growth presses hard against that border rather than
stopping on its own. The observable is min x per terrain, and the penetration
depth is border tile minus min x.

On a 200 map, left_border 40 puts the border at tile 80. Each land sits at 45
percent across, which is tile 90, so it starts inside the legal region and
grows leftward into the border. A 2000 tile round land has a radius near 25, so
without a border it would reach x = 65, which is 15 tiles past. That is enough
headroom for the decay to be visible without being so much that every value
saturates.

The four endpoints Sec.6.1 states exactly and which need no rolls at all:
  f = 0     borders ignored entirely, always accepted
  f = 100   d >= 1 never accepted, a hard stop at the border

PREDICTIONS, RECORDED BEFORE THE RUN
1. min x is ordered DIRT <= DIRT2 <= DIRT3 <= DESERT, that is penetration falls
   as f rises. If the order breaks anywhere the model is wrong in kind, not
   just in its constant.
2. DIRT at f 0 reaches roughly x 65, the unconstrained radius, showing the
   border is genuinely inert at 0.
3. DESERT at f 100 stops at exactly x 80, no tile past it. An exact 80 is the
   cleanest single reading on this map, because it is a hard boundary rather
   than a distribution, and if it comes back at 79 or 81 then Sec.4's border
   tile arithmetic is off by one and that is worth more than the fuzziness
   constant itself.
4. DIRT2 at the default f 20 penetrates a mean of a few tiles, so min x lands
   near 76 or 77. Under rev 3's rejected flat model it would have reached far
   deeper, near 65, because a flat 80 percent acceptance barely resists. The
   gap between those two is the whole reason the spec was rewritten, so this is
   the reading that either vindicates the rewrite or undoes it.

WATCH FOR
The edge is ragged by construction, so min x is a single extreme sample and
noisy. Run this one FIVE times and compare the distribution of min x, not one
value. If a cleaner instrument is wanted afterward, --rows on each terrain with
--bands 20 gives the tile count per column and the decay profile directly,
which is a much better fit to the geometric claim than an extreme is.

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --bbox DIRT
  once per terrain, then
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --rows DIRT --bands 20
*/

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

  /* f = 0, borders ignored entirely */
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type DIRT
    number_of_tiles 2000
    land_position 45 12
    left_border 40
    border_fuzziness 0
    clumping_factor 20
    base_size 2
  }

  /* f = 20, the default, and the value rev 3 and rev 4 disagree about */
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type DIRT2
    number_of_tiles 2000
    land_position 45 37
    left_border 40
    border_fuzziness 20
    clumping_factor 20
    base_size 2
  }

  /* f = 50 */
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type DIRT3
    number_of_tiles 2000
    land_position 45 62
    left_border 40
    border_fuzziness 50
    clumping_factor 20
    base_size 2
  }

  /* f = 100, hard stop. The exact-boundary reading. */
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type DESERT
    number_of_tiles 2000
    land_position 45 87
    left_border 40
    border_fuzziness 100
    clumping_factor 20
    base_size 2
  }

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>

<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

<CONNECTION_GENERATION>
